  "review": {
    "label": "curated_editorial",
    "look_ahead": "The 2024-25 season would bring a new manager, a strong start, a collapse and the Scottish Cup — the most extreme swing in the record.\n",
    "read": "This is the season where the trade-off is hardest to reduce to a verdict. The cup final and European group stage were genuine achievements; the seventh-place finish and three managers were a genuine cost. The record keeps both and refuses to use one to cancel the other.\n",
    "trade_off": "A cup final and a European group stage are exactly the kind of variance a trading model can produce: they raise revenue and expectation while consuming squad depth. The league cost was visible in the same season.\n",
    "what_did_not": "The league fell to seventh, the League Cup final was lost, and the Scottish Cup semi-final was lost on penalties. Three managers in one season is a stability cost that the cup run does not offset.\n",
    "what_worked": "The club reached a European group stage and a League Cup final, and Fletcher Boyd's debut offered a visible youth-pathway moment. The cup run and the European campaign gave the season a shape beyond the league table.\n"
  },
